---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import TopBar from '../../components/TopBar.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import CTASection from '../../components/CTASection.astro';

export const prerender = true;

const publishedDate = '2026-10-25';
const articleTitle = 'Interest Deductibility: Income Tax Folio S3-F6-C1';
const articleDescription = 'Can you deduct mortgage interest on a rental property? A plain-English guide for Ontario landlords to CRA Income Tax Folio S3-F6-C1: the use-of-funds rule, interest vs principal, mixed borrowing, and the records that protect the deduction.';
const articleUrl = 'https://www.realtorpeterlaw.com/blog/interest-deductibility';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: articleTitle,
  description: articleDescription,
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Peter Law', url: 'https://www.realtorpeterlaw.com/about' },
  publisher: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'Peter Law, Toronto Real Estate',
    logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://www.realtorpeterlaw.com/photos/peter-cn-tower.jpg', width: 1500, height: 1887 },
  },
  datePublished: publishedDate,
  dateModified: publishedDate,
  mainEntityOfPage: { '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': articleUrl },
  keywords: 'interest deductibility, Income Tax Folio S3-F6-C1, rental property mortgage interest Canada, deduct mortgage interest rental Ontario, T776 rental income, use of borrowed funds CRA, refinance rental property tax, landlord tax deductions Toronto',
};

const hreflangAlternates = [
  { hreflang: 'en-CA', href: 'https://www.realtorpeterlaw.com/blog/interest-deductibility' },
  { hreflang: 'zh-CN', href: 'https://www.realtorpeterlaw.com/chinese/blog/interest-deductibility' },
  { hreflang: 'fr-CA', href: 'https://www.realtorpeterlaw.com/french/blog/interest-deductibility' },
  { hreflang: 'x-default', href: 'https://www.realtorpeterlaw.com/blog/interest-deductibility' },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title={`${articleTitle} | Peter Law`}
  description={articleDescription}
  bodyClass="guide-page article-page"
  hreflangAlternates={hreflangAlternates}
>
  <TopBar />
  <Header active="blog" />

  <article>
    <section class="page-hero">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="eyebrow"><a href="/blog" class="back-link"><svg width="11" height="11" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="none" style="vertical-align:-1px;margin-right:5px;" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M13 7H1M6 12L1 7l5-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Blog</a> · Landlords · Tax</div>
        <h1>{articleTitle}</h1>
        <p class="article-lead">Can I deduct mortgage interest on my rental property? A plain-English guide for Ontario landlords.</p>
        <div class="article-byline">
          <span>By <a href="/about">Peter Law</a></span>
          <span class="dot">·</span>
          <time datetime={publishedDate}>October 25, 2026</time>
          <span class="dot">·</span>
          <span>5 min read</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="major" style="padding-top:32px;">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="prose-grid">
          <aside class="prose-side">
            <h2 class="side-h">On this page</h2>
            <ul>
              <li><a href="#short-answer">The short answer</a></li>
              <li><a href="#interest-only">Interest only</a></li>
              <li><a href="#the-rule">The one rule that matters</a></li>
              <li><a href="#examples">Three examples</a></li>
              <li><a href="#losing-money">Losing money on the rental?</a></li>
              <li><a href="#separate">Keep the money separate</a></li>
              <li><a href="#costly-mistake">The costliest mistake</a></li>
              <li><a href="#other-interest">What else qualifies</a></li>
              <li><a href="#if-you-sell">What if you sell?</a></li>
              <li><a href="#checklist">Quick checklist</a></li>
              <li><a href="#disclaimer">Disclaimer</a></li>
            </ul>
          </aside>

          <div class="prose-body">
            <p class="id-updated">Last updated: October 2026</p>

            <p>Most landlords know they can write off mortgage interest. Far fewer know the rule behind it. And that rule decides whether you keep the deduction or lose it.</p>
            <p>This guide explains it in plain terms.</p>

            <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer</h2>
            <div class="callout"><strong>Yes. You can usually deduct the interest on money you borrowed to buy a rental property.</strong></div>
            <p>Two things to be clear about:</p>
            <ul class="bullets">
              <li>You deduct the <strong>interest only</strong>. Never the principal.</li>
              <li>The deduction comes off your <strong>rental income</strong>, on <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/forms-publications/forms/t776.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">form T776</a>.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>This is federal tax law, not an Ontario rule. It comes from the Income Tax Act. The Canada Revenue Agency explains its position in a document called <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/technical-information/income-tax/income-tax-folios-index/series-3-property-investments-savings-plans/series-3-property-investments-savings-plan-folio-6-interest/income-tax-folio-s3-f6-c1-interest-deductibility.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Income Tax Folio S3-F6-C1, Interest Deductibility</strong></a>.</p>

            <h2 id="interest-only">Interest only. Not the whole payment.</h2>
            <p>Your mortgage payment has two parts.</p>
            <div class="id-split">
              <div class="id-split-col">
                <h3>Principal</h3>
                <p>Pays down what you owe. That is <strong>not an expense</strong>. You are paying yourself.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="id-split-col id-split-yes">
                <h3>Interest</h3>
                <p>What the bank charges you. That is an expense, and it is <strong>deductible</strong>.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p>In the early years of a mortgage, most of your payment is interest. That is why this deduction is usually a landlord's biggest write-off.</p>
            <p>Your lender sends you an annual statement showing how much interest you paid. Use that number.</p>

            <h2 id="the-rule">The one rule that matters</h2>
            <p>Here is the part people get wrong.</p>
            <div class="id-rule">
              <p><strong>CRA looks at what you did with the borrowed money. Not at what secured the loan.</strong></p>
              <p>Read that twice. The property backing the loan does not decide anything. The use of the money does.</p>
            </div>

            <h2 id="examples">Three examples</h2>
            <div class="id-examples">
              <div class="id-example id-ex-yes">
                <div class="id-example-tag">Example 1 · It works</div>
                <p>Sarah owns her home. She takes out a $200,000 line of credit against it. She uses all of it to buy a rental condo.</p>
                <p class="id-example-verdict"><strong>The interest is deductible.</strong> The loan is secured by her house, but the money bought an income property. The use is what counts.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="id-example id-ex-no">
                <div class="id-example-tag">Example 2 · It does not work</div>
                <p>Mike owns a rental condo. He refinances it and pulls out $50,000. He uses the money to renovate his own kitchen.</p>
                <p class="id-example-verdict"><strong>That $50,000 of interest is not deductible.</strong> The loan is secured by the rental, but the money went to personal use.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="id-example id-ex-messy">
                <div class="id-example-tag">Example 3 · It gets messy</div>
                <p>Priya borrows $100,000. She puts it in her everyday bank account, which already has $20,000 in it. Over the next few months she pays for a rental roof, a family vacation and a car.</p>
                <p class="id-example-verdict"><strong>She may lose part or all of the deduction.</strong> Once borrowed money is mixed with personal money, she cannot prove which dollars went where. CRA will not sort it out for her.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h2 id="losing-money">Losing money on the rental? You still get the deduction.</h2>
            <p>This surprises a lot of owners.</p>
            <p>Say your condo rents for $2,200 a month. Your mortgage, fees, taxes and insurance come to $2,600. You lose $400 a month.</p>
            <div class="callout"><strong>You can still deduct the interest.</strong></div>
            <p>The rule asks whether you expect to earn <strong>gross income</strong> from the property. Gross means total rent collected, before expenses. It does not ask whether the property turns a profit.</p>
            <p>As long as you are renting it out at a fair market rent, the interest stays deductible.</p>

            <h2 id="separate">Keep the money separate</h2>
            <p>This is the most common way landlords lose this deduction.</p>
            <div class="id-dodont">
              <div class="id-col id-do">
                <h3 class="id-col-h">Do this</h3>
                <ul>
                  <li>Open a separate bank account for the rental property</li>
                  <li>Send borrowed money straight to where it is going</li>
                  <li>Keep the loan paperwork, the bank statements and the closing documents</li>
                  <li>Keep records showing what each loan paid for</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="id-col id-dont">
                <h3 class="id-col-h">Do not do this</h3>
                <ul>
                  <li>Put borrowed money into your everyday chequing account</li>
                  <li>Pay personal bills and rental bills from the same account</li>
                  <li>Assume you will remember later</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p>If CRA asks, you need to show a clear path from the loan to the rental. That path is called a <strong>trace</strong>. Once it is broken, it is very hard to rebuild.</p>

            <h2 id="costly-mistake">The mistake that costs people the most</h2>
            <p>Pulling equity out of a rental property and spending it on yourself.</p>
            <p>People do this all the time. They refinance the rental to fund a home renovation, a car, a wedding or tuition. It feels fine because the rental is still a rental.</p>
            <p><strong>It is not fine.</strong> That portion of the interest stops being deductible. If it is all one mortgage, you now have to split the interest between the deductible part and the personal part, every year, forever.</p>
            <div class="id-honest">
              <p>If you need money for personal reasons, talk to an accountant <strong>before</strong> you borrow. There are often better ways to structure it.</p>
            </div>

            <h2 id="other-interest">What else can you deduct interest on?</h2>
            <p>Generally, interest on money borrowed for the rental business. For example:</p>
            <ul class="bullets">
              <li>Repairs and maintenance on the rental</li>
              <li>Improvements to the rental</li>
              <li>Operating costs for the rental</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Same rule applies. The money has to actually go to the rental, and you have to be able to prove it.</p>

            <h2 id="if-you-sell">What if you sell?</h2>
            <p>If you sell the rental and the sale does not cover the mortgage, you may still be able to deduct interest on the leftover debt.</p>
            <p>This comes from a separate rule in the Income Tax Act. It is sometimes called the <strong>loss of source rule</strong>.</p>
            <p>It does not apply automatically. Ask your accountant.</p>

            <h2 id="checklist">Quick checklist</h2>
            <ul class="id-checklist">
              <li><input type="checkbox" id="idc1" /><label for="idc1">Use the annual interest statement from your lender</label></li>
              <li><input type="checkbox" id="idc2" /><label for="idc2">Deduct interest only, never principal</label></li>
              <li><input type="checkbox" id="idc3" /><label for="idc3">Keep a separate bank account for the rental</label></li>
              <li><input type="checkbox" id="idc4" /><label for="idc4">Be able to show what every loan paid for</label></li>
              <li><input type="checkbox" id="idc5" /><label for="idc5">Never mix borrowed money with personal money</label></li>
              <li><input type="checkbox" id="idc6" /><label for="idc6">Talk to an accountant <strong>before</strong> refinancing, not after</label></li>
              <li><input type="checkbox" id="idc7" /><label for="idc7">Keep records for at least six years</label></li>
            </ul>

            <h2 id="remember">The one thing to remember</h2>
            <div class="id-rule">
              <p><strong>It is not about which property secured the loan. It is about what you spent the money on.</strong></p>
            </div>
            <p>Get that right, keep clean records, and the deduction is straightforward. Get it wrong, and it is expensive to fix.</p>

            <div class="id-disclaimer" id="disclaimer">
              <h3>Disclaimer</h3>
              <p>This article is general information, not tax, legal or accounting advice. Peter Law is a licensed real estate Broker with HomeLife Golconda Realty Inc., Brokerage, not an accountant or tax professional.</p>
              <p>Tax rules change and every situation is different. The examples above are simplified illustrations and do not cover every case. Before you borrow, refinance, restructure debt or file a return, speak with a Chartered Professional Accountant (CPA) or a tax lawyer who can review your specific circumstances.</p>
              <p>The authoritative sources are the <em>Income Tax Act</em> and Canada Revenue Agency Income Tax Folio S3-F6-C1, <em>Interest Deductibility</em>. Where this article and those sources differ, the sources govern. Information was current as of October 2026 and may have changed since.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="seo-tags" role="group" aria-label="Keywords">
              <h2 class="seo-tags-h">Keywords</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Interest deductibility</li>
                <li>Income Tax Folio S3-F6-C1</li>
                <li>Rental property mortgage interest Canada</li>
                <li>Deduct mortgage interest rental Ontario</li>
                <li>T776 rental income</li>
                <li>Use of borrowed funds CRA</li>
                <li>Refinance rental property tax</li>
                <li>Landlord tax deductions Toronto</li>
                <li>Loss of source rule</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>

  <CTASection heading="Own a rental, or thinking about one?" lead="I can walk you through what a Toronto rental actually earns, and connect you with accountants I trust before you borrow. Fifteen-minute call, no pitch." />
  <Footer />

  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .id-updated { font-size: 12.5px; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-mute); margin-bottom: 18px; }

  /* Principal vs interest */
  .id-split { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 14px; margin: 16px 0 18px; max-width: 700px; }
  .id-split-col { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 6px; padding: 16px 18px; }
  .id-split-col h3 { font-family: 'Fraunces', 'Fraunces Fallback', Georgia, serif; font-weight: 500; font-size: 18px; margin: 0 0 6px; color: var(--ink); }
  .id-split-col p { margin: 0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .id-split-yes { border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); }

  /* The rule */
  .id-rule { background: var(--bg-warm); border-left: 3px solid var(--ink); padding: 18px 22px; border-radius: 0 4px 4px 0; max-width: 660px; margin: 14px 0 24px; }
  .id-rule p { margin: 0 0 8px; }
  .id-rule p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .id-rule p strong { font-family: 'Fraunces', 'Fraunces Fallback', Georgia, serif; font-weight: 500; font-size: 19px; line-height: 1.35; color: var(--ink); }

  /* Examples */
  .id-examples { display: grid; gap: 14px; margin: 16px 0 22px; max-width: 760px; }
  .id-example { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 6px; padding: 16px 20px 14px; border-left-width: 4px; }
  .id-example p { margin: 0 0 8px; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.55; }
  .id-example p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .id-example-tag { font-size: 11.5px; letter-spacing: 0.16em; text-transform: uppercase; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 8px; }
  .id-ex-yes { border-left-color: #1a6b32; }
  .id-ex-yes .id-example-tag { color: #1a6b32; }
  .id-ex-no { border-left-color: #b8002e; }
  .id-ex-no .id-example-tag { color: #b8002e; }
  .id-ex-messy { border-left-color: #b26a00; }
  .id-ex-messy .id-example-tag { color: #8a5200; }
  .id-example-verdict { color: var(--ink-soft); }

  /* Do / Don't */
  .id-dodont { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 14px; margin: 16px 0 18px; max-width: 760px; }
  .id-col { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 6px; padding: 18px 20px; }
  .id-col-h { font-family: 'Fraunces', 'Fraunces Fallback', Georgia, serif; font-weight: 500; font-size: 17px; margin: 0 0 10px; padding-bottom: 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); color: var(--ink); }
  .id-col ul { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; }
  .id-col li { position: relative; padding: 5px 0 5px 22px; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .id-col li::before { position: absolute; left: 0; top: 5px; font-weight: 700; }
  .id-do li::before { content: "✓"; color: #1a6b32; }
  .id-dont li::before { content: "✕"; color: #b8002e; }

  /* Honest note */
  .id-honest { background: var(--bg-warm); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); padding: 16px 20px; border-radius: 0 4px 4px 0; max-width: 660px; margin: 14px 0 24px; }
  .id-honest p { margin: 0; }

  /* Checklist */
  .id-checklist { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 14px 0 24px; max-width: 660px; background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 6px; overflow: hidden; }
  .id-checklist li { display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px; padding: 11px 16px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .id-checklist li:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }
  .id-checklist input[type="checkbox"] { width: 18px; height: 18px; margin-top: 2px; accent-color: var(--ink); cursor: pointer; flex: 0 0 18px; }
  .id-checklist label { font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--ink); cursor: pointer; }
  .id-checklist input:checked + label { color: var(--ink-mute); text-decoration: line-through; text-decoration-thickness: 1px; }

  /* Disclaimer */
  .id-disclaimer { max-width: 660px; margin-top: 36px; padding-top: 20px; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .id-disclaimer h3 { font-size: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.18em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-mute); font-weight: 600; margin: 0 0 10px; font-family: inherit; }
  .id-disclaimer p { font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-mute); margin-bottom: 10px; }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .id-split, .id-dodont { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
  :global(html[data-theme="dark"]) .id-ex-yes { border-left-color: #5fbf7f; }
  :global(html[data-theme="dark"]) .id-ex-yes .id-example-tag,
  :global(html[data-theme="dark"]) .id-do li::before { color: #5fbf7f; }
  :global(html[data-theme="dark"]) .id-ex-no { border-left-color: #ff7a94; }
  :global(html[data-theme="dark"]) .id-ex-no .id-example-tag,
  :global(html[data-theme="dark"]) .id-dont li::before { color: #ff7a94; }
  :global(html[data-theme="dark"]) .id-ex-messy { border-left-color: #e6ad4f; }
  :global(html[data-theme="dark"]) .id-ex-messy .id-example-tag { color: #e6ad4f; }
</style>
